SORTING & SEARCHING ALGORITHMS — CHEAT SHEET
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This cheat sheet provides a quick reference to common sorting and searching algorithms, their time complexities, and use cases.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Sorting Algorithms
    1. Bubble Sort (***)
        Idea: Swap adjacent elements repeatedly
        Best For: Learning only
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:
        for i from 0 to n-1
            for j from 0 to n-i-1
                if arr[j] > arr[j+1]
                    swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
    
    Example:
       [5,1,4,2] → [1,4,2,5] → [1,2,4,5]
    
    Time: O(n²)
    Space: O(1)
    Stable: Yes

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    2. Selection Sort (***)
        Idea: Select the minimum element and place it at the beginning
        Best For: Small datasets    
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:
        for i from 0 to n-1
            minIndex = i
            for j from i+1 to n
                if arr[j] < arr[minIndex]
                    minIndex = j
            swap arr[i] and arr[minIndex]
    
    Example:
       [5,1,4,2] → [1,5,4,2] → [1,2,4,5]
    
    Time: O(n²)
    Space: O(1)
    Stable: No
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    3. Insertion Sort (*****)
        Idea: Build sorted array one element at a time
        Best For: Nearly sorted data
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:

        for i from 1 to n-1
            key = arr[i]
            j = i - 1
            while j >= 0 and arr[j] > key
                arr[j + 1] = arr[j]
                j = j - 1
            arr[j + 1] = key
    Example:
       [5,1,4,2] → [1,5,4,2] → [1,4,5,2] → [1,2,4,5]
    Time: O(n²)
    Space: O(1)
    Stable: Yes
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    4. Merge Sort (****)
        Idea: Divide and conquer, merge sorted halves
        Best For: Large datasets, stable sort
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:
        if length(arr) > 1
            mid = length(arr) / 2
            left = arr[0..mid-1]
            right = arr[mid..end]
            mergeSort(left)
            mergeSort(right)
            merge left and right into arr
    
    Example:
       [5,1,4,2] → [5,1] & [4,2] → [1,5] & [2,4] → [1,2,4,5]
    
    Time: O(n log n)
    Space: O(n)
    Stable: Yes

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    5. Quick Sort (****)
        Idea: Divide and conquer, partition around pivot
        Best For: Average case performance  
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:
        if length(arr) > 1
            pivot = arr[end]
            left = []
            right = []
            for i from 0 to length(arr)-2
                if arr[i] < pivot
                    left.append(arr[i])
                else
                    right.append(arr[i])
            return quickSort(left) + [pivot] + quickSort(right)
        else
            return arr
    Example:
       [5,1,4,2] → pivot=2 → [1] & [5,4] → [1,2,4,5]
    Time: O(n log n) average, O(n²) worst
    Space: O(log n) 
    Stable: No
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Searching Algorithms
    1. Linear Search (*****)
        Idea: Check each element sequentially
        Best For: Unsorted data, small datasets
==============================================================================
    Algorithm:
        for i from 0 to n-1
            if arr[i] == target
                return i
        return -1
    
    Example:
       arr = [5,1,4,2], target = 4 → return 2
    
    Time: O(n)
    Space: O(1)
